 WHERE...San Juan River at Pagosa Springs.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday afternoon to Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 17.0 feet, Portions of San Juan Street are beginning\nto flood.  Waters have reached the foundation of the Colorado\nRanger Building.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 5:00 PM MDT Saturday the stage was 6.6 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 8.5 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage\ntomorrow afternoon to a crest of 17.0 feet early Monday morning.\nIt will then fall below flood stage Monday afternoon.\n- Flood stage is 10.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 17.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T21:10:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.41a09bda1714f4d5c7f26254e1cfcd17fe8ea772.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.41a09bda1714f4d5c7f26254e1cfcd17fe8ea772.001.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued October 31 at 10:37AM MDT until November 2 at 12:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow expected above 10, 000 feet. Total snow\naccumulations between 12 and 18 inches, with locally higher amounts\npossible. Winds gusting as high as 45 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Northwest San Juan Mountains and Southwest San Juan\nMountains.\n\n* WHEN...From 6 AM Sunday to noon MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult to impossible. The\nhazardous conditions will impact the Monday morning commute.","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2bdd41b860e09ab541155db973dc91e9b1c8543e.001.2","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.2bdd41b860e09ab541155db973dc91e9b1c8543e.001.2"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 30 at 3:02PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From late Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-31T23:11:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 312311 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 511 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding/flash flooding concerns increase Sunday into Monday, especially for areas that have saturated soils from last week's rain. A Flood Watch remains in effect. - Forecast river responses have increased, with a number of gages in southwest Colorado exp","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&fo